> Box 5. Long-Term Risks Require Long-Term Planning: Bangladesh Case Study
>  
> Bangladesh has been identified as one of the countries with the greatest risks from climate change, with a vulnerable population exposed to multiple extreme hazards, including heat waves, rising sea-levels, and frequent flooding. In particular, Bangladesh faces higher rainfall in its wet months, potentially quadrupling the risk of extreme monsoon driving flooding by mid-century (Fahad et al. 2023, Kamruzzaman et al. 2024). These risks are already manifesting, with the worst floods in more than 100 years occurring in 2022. Studies have highlighted the disproportionate impact on women and the elderly, with potential for food insecurity and health risks (Hazrana et al. 2025). The long-term impacts of past disasters, like the 1970 cyclone, demonstrate lasting health, education, and economic adversities (Eskander & Barbier 2022).
>  
> Like many developing countries, Bangladesh aims to integrate the multiple demands of climate adaptation, economic development, and climate mitigation. Climate resilient development pathways (CRDP) has emerged as a central paradigm for doing this. CRDP recognises the role of forward-looking planning of actions when engaging in adaptation to risks that will continue evolving for decades.
>  
> Bangladesh has developed a state-of-the-art example of CRDP in the Bangladesh Delta Plan 2100 (BDP 2100). BDP 2100 is a comprehensive, multi-hazard and multi-sector plan, with the mission to “ensure long-term water and food security, economic growth, and environmental sustainability while effectively reducing vulnerability to natural disasters and building resilience to climate change and other delta challenges through robust, adaptive, and integrated strategies, and equitable water governance” (Arfanuzzaman 2024). A few of the adaptation strategies are mapped below to show key decision-points and dependences. If fully implemented, BDP 2100 is expected to eliminate a 5.6% decline in GDP that Bangladesh would otherwise experience (World Bank 2021).
> 
> More broadly, Bangladesh is notable in its approach to integrating climate adaptation and economic development. It has centered the national-level planning processes itself, producing significant documents such as the National Action Plan for Clean Cooking (2019), Sustainable Finance Policy (2020), National Strategy on Internal Displacement Management (2021), National Adaptation Plan (2022), Sector Action Plan on Environment and Climate Change (2023), and Mujib Climate Prosperity Plan (2024).
>  
> Together, the BDP 2100, National Adaptation Plan, and Mujib Climate Prosperity Plan lay out a need for $357.7 billion in funding (Arfanuzzaman 2024). This is significantly greater than what is possible for the Bangladesh government alone, which invests $4.7 billion in development, with about 50% of that provided by international aid (EDC 2020). Rather than focusing on the shortfall, Bangladesh has used its planning process as a key component of its effective approach to securing international finance. For example, this likely contributed to their receiving the largest disbursements from the UNFCCC Adaptation Fund in South Asia (Arfanuzzaman 2024).
>  
> Bangladesh’s approach to planning offers elements of a blueprint to follow their success. First, Bangladesh uses advanced, internationally recognised frameworks, like CRDP, to construct integrated perspectives. They design public-private partnerships, with project selection processes and government co-funding, as part of their plans (EDC 2020). And finally, they have created a dedicated adaptation fund, the Bangladesh Climate Change Resilience Fund, to support these projects. As an integrated approach of conceptualisation, engagement, funding, and implementation, Bangladesh’s planning process itself is a top example of adaptation in action.
